WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.230
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Wendy Brown, who's a professor of political

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.880
science at UC Berkeley

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:12.740
and also author most recently of in the ruins
of neoliberalism, the rise of anti-democratic

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:14.099
politics in the West.

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:16.470
Uh, Wendy, so great to have you on.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:17.510
Thanks for having me.

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:22.890
So first, just so we're all hopefully working
with, with the sort of same definition of

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.680
neoliberalism, I think it's a much understood
and, and, uh, much understood and debated

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.160
term in the context that you write about it.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.620
How, what are the, the important tenants that
define neo-liberalism?

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:40.030
So, just to be clear to start with, because
I do think it's confusing to many Americans

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:42.910
by neo-liberalism.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:48.690
The original neo-liberals Milton Friedman,
Friedrich kayak and others were referring

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.400
to a modification of economic liberalism.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.220
And I only want to make that clear because
still so many Americans, uh, imagine that

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:01.670
it has something to do with our notion of
political liberalism where it has to do with

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:05.400
more equality, more social spending, more
state and so forth.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.940
And it's just the opposite.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:14.640
Neoliberalism as the founding fathers of it,
uh, thought about neoliberalism meant taking

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:21.850
the basic principles of economic liberalism
that is, uh, unregulated markets maximized

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:27.890
competition and, and, and modifying those
and the modifications that they imagined and

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.960
that actually have come to be involved.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.120
Two things.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:39.130
One, understanding that markets are not necessarily
natural, even if they're good.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:40.650
So they need a lot of state support.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.930
They don't need state intervention, but they
need state support.

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:50.979
They need the state oriented to the issue
of making markets work and making markets

00:01:50.979 --> 00:01:51.979
thrive.

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:58.159
And the other thing that they did to modify
the original Adam Smith notions of, of liberalism

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:06.440
had to do with um, bringing traditional morality
into the picture.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:13.340
Understanding that not just markets but also
morals were what ought to be organizing social

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.209
life, cultural life, familial life and so
forth.

00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:21.531
And the reason I emphasize that is that so
many of us talk about neoliberalism just as

00:02:21.531 --> 00:02:26.660
a set of economic policies, but we need to
understand that it also has this other dimension,

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:31.170
a dimension that of course has really come
to the fore as we think about contemporary

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:32.170
political life.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:35.400
Yeah, I mean w what was the trajectory of
that in history?

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.340
Because it's not necessarily obvious that
what starts as a set of economic principles

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.750
would eventually come to encompass a particular
view about so-called, you know, traditional

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:51.650
family values or that it would dictate certain
foreign policy as often is a part of discussions

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.739
about neoliberal versus other points of view.

00:02:54.739 --> 00:02:59.660
What was the trajectory through which those
elements became wrapped up in this sort of

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:04.760
bigger understanding of neoliberalism in the
last 20 or 30 years?

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:13.530
The fundamental attack that neo-liberalism
mates on the social state on Keynesianism,

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:22.760
on practices of social justice that have to
do with legislation of the good, the fundamental

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.480
attack that neo-liberalism makes is this.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:35.810
It says better that what we have is just organically
developed morality and markets organizing

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:40.980
society and that means less democratic legislation.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:48.319
In fact, the neo-liberals were extremely suspicious
of and really tried to modify and, and, and

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:55.580
diminish the extent to which legislatures
in electoral democracies should do anything

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.819
other than just set general laws for people.

00:03:58.819 --> 00:04:03.750
So they really wanted legislators out of the
social justice business, out of the market

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:05.220
regulation business.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.459
And certainly out of the taxation business.

00:04:07.459 --> 00:04:10.310
So those two things come together.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:17.949
Morality and market in the attack that neo-liberals
make on democratic ways of deciding how we

00:04:17.949 --> 00:04:22.071
should live together, how much equality we
should have, how much inclusion we should

00:04:22.071 --> 00:04:28.389
have, how we ought to deal with things like
gender power, racial power, class power and

00:04:28.389 --> 00:04:29.389
so forth.

00:04:29.389 --> 00:04:36.240
All of those, the neo-liberals wanted left
alone so that traditional morality and uh,

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:42.310
markets could govern our lives and they believed
that that would maximize freedom and uh, maximize

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.080
the good, maximize a good society.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.080
Yeah.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:50.339
One of the things that, that is interesting
to me as I understand that, and I'm curious

00:04:50.339 --> 00:04:56.830
if you believe that the same kind of inherent
contradiction exists is that in a lot of the

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:04.779
ideology that's espoused about neoliberalism
and these free market, less regulation, letting,

00:05:04.779 --> 00:05:12.241
increasing freedom, the language is very much
about getting out of the way to allow more

00:05:12.241 --> 00:05:14.139
Liberty to exist.

00:05:14.139 --> 00:05:18.389
But in practice that getting out of the way
to the extent that it exists, and of course

00:05:18.389 --> 00:05:25.069
it's much more complicated than that can actually
allow a lot of different types of authoritarianism

00:05:25.069 --> 00:05:27.119
to take hold in different ways.

00:05:27.119 --> 00:05:31.110
Is that a contradiction that, that you agree
exists there?

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:35.059
Well, for them it's not a contradiction for
the following season.

00:05:35.059 --> 00:05:36.789
Uh, for us it seems that way.

00:05:36.789 --> 00:05:42.879
How, how come, what starts as a project or
a program of freedom ends up in the kind of

00:05:42.879 --> 00:05:46.580
authoritarianism that we see in the bull scenario
regime.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:51.839
Many of us argue is taking shape under Trump
and uh, err Dwan and so forth.

00:05:51.839 --> 00:05:52.839
Yes.

00:05:52.839 --> 00:05:57.379
But for, for, for insight and neoliberal frame,
the thinking goes like this.

00:05:57.379 --> 00:06:03.499
It's okay to have strong States as long as
you maximize individual Liberty.

00:06:03.499 --> 00:06:12.369
Strong States are, are the way that you will
ensure that individuals can engage in market

00:06:12.369 --> 00:06:18.860
conduct and have the moral life, the social
life that they choose.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:24.319
But what a state must not do is intervene
in individual freedoms.

00:06:24.319 --> 00:06:30.599
So to put this in, in in fairly simple language,
what Neil liberals thought was all right was

00:06:30.599 --> 00:06:34.460
a form of what we might call authoritarian
liberalism.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:40.520
That is a strong state, making decisions,
indifferent to what we'd call the checks and

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:44.180
balances and three branches of government
and all of that.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:51.889
A strong state making decisions, but leaving
alone private life, leaving alone individual

00:06:51.889 --> 00:06:52.889
life.

00:06:52.889 --> 00:06:57.849
And that's what really distinguishes the authoritarian
liberalism taking shape today and that many

00:06:57.849 --> 00:07:01.610
on the right support from classic fascism.

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:07.020
Yeah, and well, the other thing that's interesting
to me there is there's a lot of overlap between

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:11.560
that ideology and sort of what we might consider
traditional, right?

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:16.370
Libertarianism, not social libertarianism,
but right libertarianism, except when it comes

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:24.330
to foreign policy, neoliberalism has been
much more interventionist then the right libertarians

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:28.830
at least claim that they would be the, I don't
know that they've ever really gotten a shot

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:33.379
to show us how non-interventionist they would
be, but that in practice seems to be a very

00:07:33.379 --> 00:07:34.830
big distinguishing factor.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:39.999
Despite the talk of freedom and the individual
Liberty being the most important form of it.

00:07:39.999 --> 00:07:43.240
Neoliberalism has been extraordinarily interventionist
in terms of foreign policy.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:44.240
Correct.

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:49.529
Now, here again, just if I'm to try to explain
what neo-liberals think they're doing, what

00:07:49.529 --> 00:07:54.719
they're trying, what they imagine they're
trying to do is create a world of global free

00:07:54.719 --> 00:07:55.719
trade.

00:07:55.719 --> 00:08:00.529
Now what's happening right now is another
story with the trade Wars and so forth.

00:08:00.529 --> 00:08:05.110
But with the classic Neil liberal, uh, foreign
policy advocates thought they were doing,

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:09.099
for example, in Iraq regime change in Iraq.

00:08:09.099 --> 00:08:15.020
And then Neil liberalize the society, make
it into part of the global order of, among

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:18.979
other things, debt financing and free trade
and so forth.

00:08:18.979 --> 00:08:27.479
So you're absolutely right that they understand,
uh, a highly interventionist foreign policy

00:08:27.479 --> 00:08:30.490
as part of what is bringing about the Neo
liberal order.

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:36.870
But there's one other area that I would emphasize
where Neo, uh, the, the kind of markets and

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:42.839
morals program of the neo-liberals seems to
really intervene in individual Liberty.

00:08:42.839 --> 00:08:49.750
And that's of course, um, reproductive freedom
and LGBT rights.

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:56.069
And again, the official thinking here is,
well, that has to do with the moral order.

00:08:56.069 --> 00:09:02.660
These things go against traditional morality,
which is tried and true and belongs part and

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:05.089
parcel with a free market society.

00:09:05.089 --> 00:09:10.080
So it's slippery there, but there you really
do have a contradiction, lots of intervention

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:22.139
in the freedom of um, uh, women and uh, non-normative
uh, sexualities saying no, you can't do that.

00:09:22.139 --> 00:09:30.139
There is the, I guess we could call it the
hope among many on the American left that

00:09:30.139 --> 00:09:35.879
socially democratic ideas that are similar
to those of the Scandinavian economies, Northern

00:09:35.879 --> 00:09:42.710
Europe, et cetera, are experiencing, um, maybe
a resurgence or Renaissance of sorts or, or

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:47.569
maybe approaching something new that maybe
we've not even seen before in the United States

00:09:47.569 --> 00:09:54.930
and that the rise of those ideas will necessarily
push out some of the neoliberal framework

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:57.980
that has been the status quo for a long time.

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:01.649
Is it as clear as if one goes up, the other
goes down?

00:10:01.649 --> 00:10:03.220
Is it more complicated than that?

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:07.560
Do you even agree that that's actually a trend
that we're now seeing?

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:13.839
I do think that there has been a fairly robust
challenge to Neo liberal ideas really since

00:10:13.839 --> 00:10:16.890
the 2008 finance capital meltdown.

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:25.060
I mean that was the beginning of the end of
a broad endorsement of free markets privatization,

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:34.930
uh, minimal social state depression of progressive
taxation that post 2008 rethink went beyond

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:39.699
occupy, although occupy really embodied it
that, uh, you know, and what we need to remember

00:10:39.699 --> 00:10:47.130
is that however limited the actual timeframe
of occupy wall street, what it did was begin

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:54.209
to change the conversation about inequality,
about access to public education, higher education,

00:10:54.209 --> 00:11:00.689
uh, about banks being given free reign to
do whatever they wanted, bailing out the banks

00:11:00.689 --> 00:11:03.739
but not the foreclosed homeowners and so forth.

00:11:03.739 --> 00:11:11.439
So with that conversation now changed, uh,
you get the rise of Bernie Sanders and in

00:11:11.439 --> 00:11:19.069
turn AOC and Elizabeth Warren and so forth
such that I, I would actually agree that what

00:11:19.069 --> 00:11:25.420
you have is a fairly robust challenge now
to Neil the economic ideas.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:31.380
But, uh, I would say a little bit less savvy
on the part of the left about the extent to

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:38.339
which neoliberalism is also bound up with
this idea that the state should stay out of

00:11:38.339 --> 00:11:44.130
the moral realm by which is meant the social
justice realm.

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:51.170
Again, the left has certainly been robust
on the subject of arguing for gender rights,

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:56.940
LGBT rights, black civil rights, um, endorsing
all kinds of social movement.

00:11:56.940 --> 00:12:02.959
But I, but I do think it's the part of the
story that's more complicated is taking apart

00:12:02.959 --> 00:12:12.170
that broad belief that neo-liberalism set
into our, the bones of society, that it's

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:18.589
best for the state to be out of the business
of social justice redistribution, social spending

00:12:18.589 --> 00:12:19.870
and all the rest.

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:23.189
And to just let those things be handled individually.

00:12:23.189 --> 00:12:26.610
So to put a fine, to compress the point.

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:33.329
I do think that we have a pretty major challenge
now to neoliberal economic thinking, but maybe

00:12:33.329 --> 00:12:39.300
not a full grasp of the way that what I call
neoliberal reason has, has really come to

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:45.319
organize a lot of our understanding of society
and what's appropriate for democratic legislation.

00:12:45.319 --> 00:12:46.319
Yeah.

00:12:46.319 --> 00:12:50.550
I've also found the challenge to be less robust
when it comes to foreign policy.

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:53.950
I mean, you do hear, Hey, we, we shouldn't
be getting it.

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:55.360
We're not the world's policemen.

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:56.490
We should be getting out of here.

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:57.550
They're the other place.

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:00.680
But it's been a less sort of nuanced and well
thought out challenge.

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:02.190
I don't know if you would agree.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:03.230
I totally agree.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:09.759
And I think that in many ways in the United
States, Trump has really confused the left

00:13:09.759 --> 00:13:15.860
in its own rethinking its own thinking about
foreign policy because he's, Dan's now for

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:21.199
what the anti Imperial left used to stand
for, which is get out of the endless Wars,

00:13:21.199 --> 00:13:22.420
don't be the world's policemen.

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:30.069
Of course he's trying to do all kinds of things
at the level of, of trade and still engaged

00:13:30.069 --> 00:13:38.620
in, in um, massive, what we could call neo-colonial
condemnation of all kinds of nations and practices.

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:45.720
But the, the withdrawal that has typically
been the stance of many on the left has, has

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:54.439
really, I think not been fully come to terms
with by the left to think about, okay, where,

00:13:54.439 --> 00:14:00.399
where does the lift stand on the question
of joining together with others in the world,

00:14:00.399 --> 00:14:06.380
not just to solve climate change, not just
to get institutions of political economy,

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:10.189
right, but also to right wrongs, where are
we with human rights diff courts, where are

00:14:10.189 --> 00:14:12.800
we with humanitarian intervention?

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:16.680
And the left has gone super quiet on those
subjects.

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:17.779
Absolutely fascinating.

00:14:17.779 --> 00:14:22.639
We've been talking with Wendy Brown, who's
professor of political science at UC Berkeley,

00:14:22.639 --> 00:14:24.370
most recently, author of [inaudible]

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:30.380
in the ruins of neoliberalism, the rise of
anti-democratic politics in the West.

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:31.600
Wendy's such a pleasure having you on.

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:32.600
I appreciate your time, David.

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:34.010
It was good to talk to you.

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:34.430
Thank you.

